From 5922b6941e2d6c00fbb983d11d293cc1c56bdc7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 07:23:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Keep the bundled blocker when its file cannot be prepared

BareBundledExtensionsLoader returned an empty dictionary whenever it could
not get the crx out of the apk: no user data directory, a directory it could
not create, an asset it could not open or read, a file it could not write. The
external provider reads an empty dictionary as "this extension has been
withdrawn", and ExternalProviderManager::CheckExternalUninstall then uninstalls
it as an orphan, settings and all. A full disk or one failed read cost the user
their blocker and everything they had set in it. A new uBlock Origin version is
the likeliest moment for it, since that is when a new file has to be written.

A failure now keeps claiming the extension, as something to keep rather than
something to install. keep_if_present stops the provider installing anything
when it is absent, and an external_version of 0 is older than any installed
copy, so nothing is reinstalled over it from a file that may not be there. The
provider only counts an entry carrying both external_crx and external_version
as a claim, which is why the entry still names a path. The next launch tries
the file again. Leaving the blocker out on purpose is still how declining it on
the welcome screen works.

Two smaller things in the same function. The crx used to be trusted as soon as
a file of that name existed, so a launch interrupted halfway through writing it
left a truncated file every later launch would reuse; it is now written beside
the target and renamed into place, and an existing file is only reused if it is
the asset byte for byte. And the decision itself now lives in
components/bare_bundled_extensions, which depends on //base only, so its cases
have unit tests that run on the host.
